exp-23: compress the no-list 64-bit class and read it through R64_UINT

Experiment rig.  NOT FOR SHIP: it is a measurement hook with an environment
switch, it has no business in a released driver, and the tree is reverted with
`git apply -R` and explicit file names after the run (AGENTS.md: never
git checkout/restore in ../mesa27-src).

The question.  The 64-bit compatibility class is the last class whose UBWC has
never been asked, and the member still missing from every measurement is
R64_UINT.  It is missing for a structural reason, and that reason decides the
shape of the question:

  - a format list that NAMES R64_UINT can never be compressed.
    fd6_ubwc_compat_mode() has no case for R64_UINT/R64_SINT, so the member
    answers FD6_UBWC_UNKNOWN_COMPAT, and
    tu6_mutable_format_list_ubwc_compatible() refuses any list holding an
    UNKNOWN member before it compares labels (tu_formats.cc).  No knob that
    folds two known labels changes that - which is why exp-22 could not ask it
    either, and why probe XR64's LST64 row is expected to print a refusal;
  - R64_UINT has no texture and no colour path.  tu_formats.cc grants it
    STORAGE_IMAGE, STORAGE_IMAGE_ATOMIC and the transfer features under
    props.has_64b_image_atomics (true for a7xx_gen2) and asserts that it has no
    vertex, colour or texture entry, so the only reader it has is a storage
    image;
  - an image created WITHOUT a list is viewable as R64_UINT as soon as its usage
    carries STORAGE, and that is the only create where the view is legal and the
    image could be compressed.

So the question has to be asked of a no-list create with STORAGE usage, by a
storage reader, and nothing else.  That is what probe XR64 is
(tests/rp6-vkd3d-sparse-test.c, probe_xr64/xr64_storage): fill by a
buffer->image copy through the base format, read every 64-bit word through a
r64ui storage image (xr64_load_spv), then imageAtomicAdd(+1) per word
(xr64_atomic_spv) and read again - so a wrong answer is a wrong value, not a
lost write, and XMC's own DETECT row stays the check that the comparison can
see wrong data at all.

The knob.  TU_UBWC_NOLIST_64=1 stops the no-list mutable branch of
tu_image_init() from dropping UBWC, and nothing else.  It cannot widen a shape
nobody asked about: an image that carries a format list, or whose base texel
block is not 8 bytes, or one where ubwc_possible() already said no, is
untouched - the hook declines a demotion, it never grants compression a base
format cannot have.  The layout decision
(tu6_mutable_format_list_forces_linear) is left alone, so the hook cannot move
an image between tiled and linear: with the knob off the same create is the
control for the same create with the knob on, in one driver binary.  is_mutable
stays false, which is the point of the shape: the compressor is asked about an
image the application is still free to reinterpret through a 64-bit view - the
same asymmetry that makes the no-list classes interesting and that 0014
deliberately does not touch ("UBWC is deliberately not touched").

How it is run.  One driver binary, three runs of the same test:

    source scripts/env.sh
    export RP6_TEST_BIN=$WORK/rp6-vkd3d-sparse-test
    scripts/run-test.sh build/p14-shipped/freedreno_icd_p14.json \
        results/raw/xr64-p14.log
    scripts/run-test.sh build/out-exp23/freedreno_icd_exp23.json \
        results/raw/xr64-exp23-knoboff.log
    TU_UBWC_NOLIST_64=1 scripts/run-test.sh \
        build/out-exp23/freedreno_icd_exp23.json results/raw/xr64-exp23-knobon.log

The released stack is the outside reference (no knob in it); the knob-off run of
the experiment binary is the control for the knob-on run.  Rows: NOL64 (no list,
STORAGE in usage - the create the hook grants), SGL64 (single-member list, which
keeps UBWC today without any knob, so it says whether a compressed 64-bit image
survives this fill and this reader at all), LST64 (a list that NAMES R64_UINT -
the refusal, as a check on the model rather than as a question).

Reading the answer.  On the knob-on run NOL64 must first show the metadata tail
(tail=0x2000, the signature UBR, CEIL and XMC use) or it says nothing about
compression at all: a reserved plane is not a compression claim either way
(journal 22.2), and that warning is the whole reason XMC's rows are quoted as
EXACT rather than as MATCH.  Then, for the same create:

  load EXACT and atomic+load EXACT  -> a UBWC-compressed 64-bit image is readable
                         and writable through the one view no other reader can
                         provide.  exp-18 (b) gains a form that is both narrow
                         and backed by a measurement, and the 650.8 MiB of
                         no-list 64-bit bytes journal 28 counts become a question
                         worth a ship patch and a game run.
  load EXACT, atomic MISMATCH       -> the read path agrees and the write path
                         does not; that is a UBWC coherency/atomic question on
                         gen2, not a compat-table question, and it is a stop.
  load MISMATCH                     -> compression changes what the storage read
                         sees.  The no-list grant is unsafe for this class, and
                         the direction closes with a measurement instead of a
                         prediction (the way 0014 v2 and exp-10 closed theirs).

Neither result is a ship rule and neither says anything about the game's fps:
the write paths into such an image (clear, blit, r2d) are not what this probe
runs, and the value of the class in this workload is journal 28's byte count,
not a measurement of traffic.

Applies on top of exp-21 (the tree carries 0001..0013 + exp-14 + exp-21; 0014 is
deliberately not applied, results/test-exp21-equiv-p14.log shows why that is the
right base).  Touches one file, src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_image.cc.

